#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-groups.md:1
msgid "Behavior groups"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-groups.md:3
msgid "A **behavior** (a behavior group) is a named set of relationships that belong together: _Adaptive lamp_ = `dimByTilt` + `brightness`. It is a way of **organising and seeing** a design — a region on the canvas that can be collapsed to one box — and it changes **nothing about what the design does**."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-groups.md:8
msgid "That sentence is the whole point. Grouping is not a semantic operation. The values, the timing, the outputs, the simulation trace and the deployment placement are identical before and after you group, ungroup, move a relationship in or out, or collapse a box. A group has no formula, no domain, no ports."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-groups.md:13
msgid "Groups exist in every project ([System projects](../studio/system-projects.md))."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-groups.md:15
msgid "What a group is for"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-groups.md:17
msgid "**Reading** a design with many relationships: the canvas shows _Adaptive lamp_ as one box with what goes in and what comes out."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-groups.md:19
msgid "**Managing** related relationships together: move them as one, name the idea they implement, describe it."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-groups.md:21
msgid "**Semantic zoom**: zoomed out, every behavior reads as its summary box; zoomed in, the members are visible inside the region."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/concepts/behavior-groups.md:23
msgid "**The step before packaging**: a group is what you turn into a [component](components.md) — but a group is not a component, and most groups never become one."
msgstr ""

