#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:1
msgid "Syntax basics"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:3
msgid "What a `.bdl` file looks like and the forms that are checked today. Everything here parses; the _Checked?_ notes say whether it is also checked and runs. The normative grammar is `docs/spec/textual-syntax.md`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:7
msgid "A file"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:9
msgid "A file is a sequence of **items**. Items start with a keyword and end where the next keyword starts; newlines carry no meaning; `//` and `/* … */` are comments. A project is any number of files under `src/`, read in path order; an item may refer to anything declared in any file."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:39
msgid "`Type` names the value form: `Bool`, `Count`, or a quantity kind — `Scalar` (no unit), `Angle`, `Length`, `Time`, `Mass`, `Current`, `Temperature`, `Amount`, `Luminous`, and derived kinds such as `Speed`, `Frequency`, `Force`, `Pressure`, `Voltage`, `Illuminance`. Leaving the type off declares a concept whose value form is decided later."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:54
msgid "The definition repeats the name and gives one parameter per input. A relationship that reads nothing is written `mapping level : () -> Brightness` and defined `level() = …`: `()` is the empty domain, and `() -> Brightness` is the relationship's type. Older files write the same thing as `mapping level : Brightness`; that shorthand still opens and means the same declaration, the editor marks it with a hint — _A relationship with no inputs is written explicitly as `() -> Brightness`. The output-only shorthand is deprecated._ — with the fix **Make empty domain explicit**, and `bdld migrate-unit-domain <project>` rewrites a whole project when you ask; the formatter never rewrites it for you. Two inputs may be spelled `(A, B) -> C` as well as `A -> B -> C`. (`()` is not a measurement unit and not `_`.) Without a definition, the relationship is _declared_ — an input, or work still to do. `@domain` after the signature puts the relationship in a timing domain ([Timing domains](../concepts/timing.md)); without it the relationship serves any domain."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:70
msgid "**Checked?** Yes. The parameter names are the names the body uses: `dimByTilt(t) = t / (90 deg)` reads its input as `t`. Renaming the concept does not change `t`; renaming the parameter is an edit of the definition."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:74
msgid "Descriptions"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:76
msgid "A run of `///` lines directly above an item is its description — the text Studio shows in the sheet and in _Explain_. Ordinary `//` and `/* … */` comments are yours and are kept where you wrote them."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:85
msgid "Timing domains, outputs, drivers, devices"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:99
msgid "A physical output names the concept it accepts and its domain; `optional` marks one that may stay undriven. `drive light by brightness` says that `brightness` drives the logical output `light` — the output is driven by the relationship, which is the whole of the relation; a second `drive` of the same output is a finding, and the first stays. Older files write `drive light = brightness`; that spelling still opens and means the same drive, the editor marks it with a hint — _Prefer `by` for output driving_ — with the fix **Write `by`**, and `bdld migrate-drive-by <project>` rewrites a whole project when you ask; the formatter never rewrites it for you. A device has a kind — `pwm_channel`, `digital_output`, `h_bridge_channel`, `i2c_sensor`, `quadrature_encoder`, `uart` — optionally the output it realises (`for light`) and pins fixed by position (`pin 0 = D3`)."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:112
msgid "**Checked?** Yes: drivers are checked as in Studio (one driver, right concept and domain); devices take part in deployment analysis."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:115
msgid "Components, instances, bindings"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:148
msgid "Inside a component body the ordinary items apply (`concept`, `mapping`, `clock`, `output`, `drive`, `device`); a concept declared _in_ the body is private to each instance, a `use concept` is the project's concept shared by all. `requires`, `provides` and `param` declare ports — each is a relationship of the body with a contract other components can rely on ([Components](../concepts/components.md)). An instance gives every clock parameter a domain and every `param` a constant, in braces. `bind` connects an instance's port to a relationship or to another instance's port; when the two sides update in different domains, `init` gives the value before the source has one."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:159
msgid "**Checked?** Yes — composition is validated as in Studio: a port bound to the wrong concept, an instance missing a parameter, a domain crossing without `init`, are findings on the line concerned."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:163
msgid "Expressions"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:165
msgid "Checked?"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:167
msgid "numbers, with a unit"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:167
msgid "`90 deg`, `2.5 s`, `300 lx`, `0.5`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:167
msgid "yes — units are checked as dimensions; unknown units are reported"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:168
msgid "arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:168
msgid "`a + b`, `a - b`, `a * b`, `a / b`, `-a`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:169
msgid "`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `==`, `!=` (no chaining: write `a < b && b < c`)"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:170
msgid "logic"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:170
msgid "`&&`, `\\|\\|`, `!`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:171
msgid "`if c then a else b`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:172
msgid "call a relationship"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:172
msgid "yes — with the right number of arguments; a rule as a _value_ or a partial call is refused"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:173
msgid "name a value"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:173
msgid "`tilt`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:174
msgid "block with `let`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:174
msgid "`{ let x = …; let y = …; x + y }`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:176
msgid "`match`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:176
msgid "on true/false, options, whole numbers, and a concept through its value"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:176
msgid "yes, with exhaustiveness checking"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:177
msgid "`delay(init, e)`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:177
msgid "yes — in a value's formula; at the top level or in a `let`, an `if` branch or a `match` scrutinee"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:178
msgid "cross-domain read"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:178
msgid "`sync(domain, init, e)`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:178
msgid "yes — same placement rule"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:179
msgid "`enum` items and their constructors"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:179
msgid "`enum Mode { Off, Manual(Brightness) }`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:179
msgid "parses; reported as _open_ — not usable in formulas yet"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:180
msgid "a relationship's parameter"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:180
msgid "`t` in `dimByTilt(t) = t / (90 deg)`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:180
msgid "yes — the parameter name is the name; the concept's name is not needed"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:181
msgid "generic types in a signature"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:181
msgid "`Option<Brightness>`"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:181
msgid "parses; meaning decided by the checker"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:183
msgid "Units attach to numbers only: `90 deg` is a literal; `x deg` is a syntax error. A dimensioned expression gets its dimension from arithmetic: `(tilt + offset) / (90 deg)`."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:187
msgid "Patterns"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:189
msgid "`_`, a name, `true` / `false`, a whole number, `Some(p)`, `None`. No guards, ranges or or-patterns."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:192
msgid "Where Studio and text differ"
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:194
msgid "The formula field in Studio is the expression language above — nothing more, nothing less. What the file adds is the items around it. Two things Studio has that the text does not carry: **behavior groups** and **canvas layout** — both are authoring metadata, kept in files beside the sources ([Project files](../reference/project-files.md)). `enum` items parse but are reported as _open_: the design model has no sum types yet. The words `context` and `require` are reserved."
msgstr ""

#: docs/user-guide/textual/syntax-basics.md:204
msgid "[Overview and current status](overview.md) · [Editor and language server](editor-and-lsp.md) · [Authoring a project as text](../workflows/authoring-as-text.md) · [Formula language](../reference/formula-language.md)"
